:: meera_full_plan_d2
<<img "kavya_meera_planning.png" "Room 304, plan session">>
Priya left at 6:50   Dr. Pathak's biochemistry, which she attends with religious devotion because he "has nice forearms when he rolls up his sleeves." The door closes. Footsteps recede.

Meera locks it. Bolt. Chain. Then sits, spine straight   dancer's posture even in a cotton nightie at 7 AM planning a conspiracy that would give her father an aneurysm.

<div class="dialogue">"Arjun swaps with you." No preamble. Meera doesn't do preambles. "He comes into the hostel in your body. I get to be with him   touch him, talk to him, be in the same room as my boyfriend for the first time in six months. You go out in his body. To VIT. To his classes."</div>

Your brain is already running the algorithm. Input: two bodies, two minds. Operation: swap.

"Mere body mein woh   a boy   will be walking around the hostel. Sleeping in MY bed. Using MY bathroom. Meera, tere saath   woh mere "<span class="gloss-en">A boy will be walking around the hostel in my body.</span>

<div class="dialogue">"Focus, Kavya. You get to attend his CS classes. VIT. Full day. Real lectures. Real labs. A keyboard that isn't three inches wide."</div>

The words land in your solar plexus. Not a punch   a key turning in a lock.

CS classes. Not MIT OCW buffering at 240p. A real classroom, a real professor who published a real paper on optimal BST construction that you read during pharmacology and wept internally at.

<div class="dialogue">"He'll have to PEE. In my body. He'll SEE everything. He'll have to   the BRA, Meera. Do you know what it takes to put on a bra from the inside?"</div>

<div class="dialogue">"I'll teach him. I've been wearing bras since I was thirteen."</div>

You walk to the window. VIT is out there, northwest, in Bibwewadi. C-Block lecture halls. A whole department dedicated to the thing you actually love.

You turn back. "Rules. Non-negotiable."

<<run setup.flag("meera_plan_heard")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: swap_rules_d2
You pull out the Redmi. Open Notes. Your thumbs move at the velocity of a woman drafting a legal contract while her best friend waits with the patience of someone who knows the contract is the price of admission.

<div class="sensory">
<b>SWAP RULES v1.0</b>
1. NO sexual activity in Kavya's body without explicit written permission. Not even "accidental."
2. Kavya gets FULL access to Arjun's CS schedule, materials, lab accounts, library card.
3. Swap minimum 3 hours, maximum 12.
4. Emergency swap-back via the brass bell   panic button, range ~2 km. No exceptions.
5. If Priya gets suspicious   ABORT EVERYTHING.
6. Nobody else knows. NOBODY. Not Rohit. Not parents. Not the chai wala.
7. Full debrief after every swap. Complete account of both bodies. No omissions.
8. Kavya retains VETO over any activity involving her body she hasn't pre-approved.
9. If menstruation occurs during a swap   IMMEDIATE swap-back. No discussion.
</div>

Meera reads over your shoulder. She smells of Parachute coconut oil and last night's sandalwood. "Fair," she says.

"One more thing." You lower your voice   for weight, not secrecy. "If he does anything to my body I haven't approved, the swaps stop permanently. And his Pulsar doesn't start for a month."

<div class="dialogue">"Your promise isn't the one I need, Meera. His is."</div>

<div class="dialogue">"You'll hear it from him. Tomorrow. Before the swap. Face to face."</div>

You save the document. Lock the phone. Slide it under the pillow.

<<run setup.flag("swap_rules_established")>>
<<run setup.addItem("swap_rules_doc")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: schedule_study_d2
Meera pulls up VIT's public course portal on the Redmi. Hostel WiFi is a conceptual framework, not a utility, but eventually the page resolves. Third Year CSE, Section A, tomorrow:

<div class="sensory">
09:45   Data Structures &amp; Algorithms (Prof. R. Krishnan, C-204)
11:00   Operating Systems (Prof. S. Desai, C-207)
14:00   Algorithm Design Tutorial (optional, Lab 3)
</div>

"Prof. Krishnan published a paper on optimal BST construction last year," you hear yourself say, quiet   the frequency you use in temples and libraries. "He reduced the time complexity from O(n³) to O(n² log n) with a novel recurrence. I spent two days verifying it on paper during pharmacology."

Meera watches you. She's seen you at your worst in anatomy lab, seen you lie to your father about loving medicine. But this naked wanting for a lecture hall and a whiteboard   this she's never seen, because you've never let it show.

<div class="dialogue">"So you'll meet the man who wrote it. In person. As his student."</div>

"Fine." Your eyes open. "Call him tonight."

<<run setup.flag("schedule_memorized")>>
<<hubreturn>>


