<<hubreturn>>


:: chant_practice_d2
Priya's out. Door locked. Meera sits in padmasana, the Bharatanatyam training making the hip rotation look effortless when you know from anatomy that it's borderline orthopedic.

<div class="dialogue">"Sixteen syllables. //Om deha-vinimaya-siddhi-da namah Shivaya.//<span class="gloss-en">Om, to the accomplished power of body-exchange, salutations to Shiva.</span> Sanskrit is phonetic. Mispronunciation isn't wrong-sounding   it IS wrong. The vibration pattern changes."</div>

"You've turned a fourteenth-century tantric ritual into an electrical-engineering metaphor."

<div class="dialogue">"I've been rooming with you for three years. Some things rub off."</div>

She chants. The syllables roll off her tongue, crisp and ancient. You follow. Your Sanskrit is functional   compulsory university paper, the linguistic equivalent of driving an automatic while she runs manual. Your //deha// is flat where hers curves.

<div class="dialogue">"Softer on the 'sh'. Palatal, not alveolar. Tongue to the hard palate, not the tooth ridge."</div>

"Meera, I'm a medical student. I know exactly what the hard palate is. I just can't make my tongue do the right thing with it."

Twenty minutes. By repetition sixty, something happens   not the buzzing of the test swap, but a resonance. The syllables fitting. Like code compiling without errors.

<<run setup.flag("chant_practiced")>>
<<run setup.stat("trust_meera", 2, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: pharmacology_lecture_d2
<<img "kavya_pharmacology.png" "Pharmacology hall">>
<<if setup.clock() lt 570>>\
You're early. You sit outside on a bench while the hall fills, dupatta over your nose out of habit even though there's no formalin here   just the smell of chalk and forty MBBS students dreading the same hour. By 9:30 it starts.
<<run setup.advanceTime(570 - setup.clock())>>
<<beat>>
<</if>>\
Dr. Mhatre stands at the podium   thin, wire glasses, the energy of a man who has never once questioned whether memorising the mechanism of seventeen beta-blockers is a reasonable ask. His pointer sweeps a slide with no fewer than forty bullet points: //Pharmacokinetics   Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion.//

You know this the way you know every medical subject   thoroughly, accurately, with the emotional engagement of someone reading a software EULA.

<div class="intimate"><span class="tag">Intimate · body awareness</span>
The plastic chair digs into your thighs. The bra underwire presses differently at this angle   a point against your left ribcage you've been negotiating since you sat. You cross your legs. Uncross them. The salwar bunches between your thighs, cotton on cotton, friction you've worn every day for three years and suddenly can't stop feeling.

Tomorrow: no bra. No dupatta pinned across your chest. No drawstring at your waist. Jeans and a t-shirt. From the //outside.//
</div>

<div class="dialogue">"Miss Iyer. The therapeutic window of digoxin   narrow or wide?"</div>

"Narrow, sir. 0.5 to 2.0 nanograms per mL. Toxicity: nausea, yellow halos, cardiac arrhythmias."

<div class="dialogue">"Correct."</div> The class exhales.

You write in the margin, very small: //Tomorrow I learn if a body is a vehicle or a cage.//

<<run setup.flag("in_pharma_lecture")>>
<<run setup.flag("college_survived_d2")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: mhatre_test_d2
<<if setup.has("surprise_test_passed")>>\
Your paper is already in the pile. Mhatre glances at you over his glasses on his way past. He doesn't say anything. He just makes a note.
<<hubreturn>>
<<else>>\
Dr. Mhatre enters carrying the harbinger of suffering: a stack of papers, face-down, corners aligned.

<div class="dialogue">"Close your notebooks. Phones away. This is an unannounced assessment."</div>

Sixty MBBS students groan   the documented groan of the unprepared meeting the inevitable. But Sneha warned you yesterday, and you //built// the dosage calculator, which means the formulas are in your head, cross-referenced and debugged.

Ten questions, thirty minutes. Clark's formula, Cockcroft–Gault, loading doses for aminoglycosides, renal-impairment adjustments. Math with medical labels. The CS brain activates   functions, variables, inputs, outputs.

You finish in twelve minutes. Re-check in four. Sit the remaining fourteen watching classmates sweat, thinking about Prof. Krishnan's BST paper.

<div class="dialogue">"Miss Iyer. You finished remarkably fast. Most students can't spell Cockcroft without checking."</div>

"I had practice, sir."

He makes a note. You don't care what it says. In eighteen hours you won't be in this chair.

<<run setup.flag("surprise_test_passed")>>
<<run setup.stat("med_knowledge", 3, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>
<</if>>


:: margin_coding_d2
Mhatre's voice drones about hepatic first-pass metabolism. Under the desk, in the margin of your notebook, you sketch the control flow for the Sanskrit-loanword tokenizer   the sandhi-splitting logic, the dhatu recognition. Nobody looks. Nobody ever looks. You've been hiding code in medical notebooks for three years.

<<run setup.stat("coding_skill", 1, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: sneha_pharma_d2
<<if setup.has("study_group_joined")>>\
Sneha gives you a thumbs up across the hall. "Wednesday. Library. 6 PM. Don't forget."
<<hubreturn>>
<<else>>\
Sneha drops into the seat beside you, still glowing from the surprise test.

<div class="dialogue">"Kavya! The dosage app saved my LIFE. 8 out of 10. I've never scored 8 on anything except Instagram followers per week."</div>

"Glad it helped."

<div class="dialogue">"Listen   I'm starting a pharmacology study group. Me, Reshma, Aarti. Wednesday evenings, library. You should come."</div>

A study group for a subject you'll be studying for approximately zero future hours if Meera's plan works. But the optics are perfect   Kavya Iyer joins a study group. Normal behaviour. Priya-approved behaviour. Camouflage.

"I'm in."

<div class="dialogue">"Wednesday. Library. 6 PM. Bring your brain."</div>

Your brain will be in a different body by Wednesday. But sure.

