:: last_coding_d2
<<img "kavya_last_coding.png" "Library, Terminal 6">>
<<if setup.has("last_kavya_coding")>>\
Terminal 6. You add a few more test cases, save to the USB, clear the history. The Dell doesn't care that this was supposed to be goodbye.
<<hubreturn>>
<<else>>\
The library computer section. Terminal 6. Your terminal. The membrane keyboard you've memorised down to the specific mushiness of each key, the delay on the spacebar, the Enter key that needs a full press.

The GitHub issue assigned to KV_Codes this morning: Sanskrit loanword tokenization. Hindi absorbs Sanskrit words but modifies their declension. The tokenizer needs to recognise the root   the //dhatu//   and split compounds at sandhi junctions.

Your fingers begin. The code flows. The monitor glows with the warm syntax highlighting of code that works. You're not Kavya Iyer, MBBS student. You're KV_Codes. The person three professional developers called elegant.

Forty minutes. The tokenizer handles the loanwords correctly   splitting compounds at sandhi junctions, tagging roots. You test it on a paragraph of the Bhagavad Gita and it performs beautifully.

This is the last time you code as Kavya. Tomorrow you code as Arjun. Same brain, different hands.

You save. USB drive. Clear history. Stand up. In the black monitor: maroon kameez, wavy hair, dark circles, beauty mark.

"Goodbye," you tell Terminal 6. "I'll be back. But I'll be different."

<<run setup.flag("last_kavya_coding")>>
<<run setup.stat("coding_skill", 3, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>
<</if>>


:: priya_gossip_d2
Priya bursts in sweating and chattering   her twin natural states. She dumps her towel, grabs her water bottle, and launches in without preamble.

<div class="dialogue">"Kavya, Room 308 ki Anjali? Her boyfriend   Siddharth, the Fergusson guy with the man-bun   he SNUCK INTO THE HOSTEL. Last week."</div>

Your ears recalibrate. Intel incoming.

<div class="dialogue">"Kitchen window! The one at the back into the alley behind the mess. The kitchen chowkidar   Ramesh ka assistant, the young one, Ganesh   takes a chai break EVERY EVENING. 7:30 to 8:00. Without fail. Thirty minutes of zero surveillance on the back entrance."</div>

You file it the way you file a buffer-overflow exploit. 7:30 to 8:00 PM. Kitchen window. Broken latch. Unmonitored.

<div class="dialogue">"Nobody reported it. There's an unwritten code   we don't snitch on each other for boyfriend stuff."</div>

The word //code// hangs there. Priya means slang. You hear protocol. The hostel has formal rules enforced by Mrs. Joshi and informal ones enforced by solidarity   and the informal ones protect exactly the kind of thing you're about to do at a scale Anjali never imagined.

<<run setup.flag("priya_gossip_heard")>>
<<run setup.flag("hostel_security_intel")>>
<<run setup.flag("kitchen_gap_known")>>
<<run setup.stat("rel_priya", 2, "kavya")>>
<<run setup.stat("priya_suspicion", -2, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: anjali_d2
<<img "room_308.png" "Room 308">>
You knock on 308. Anjali opens   compact, dark ponytail, the alert baseline of someone who's been on the edge of expulsion for two weeks.

<div class="dialogue">"Priya told me about last week."</div>

Her face cycles: alarm, resignation, calculation. "She needs to learn to shut up."

"I'm not telling anyone. I need the layout. The kitchen back area. The window."

Her eyes narrow, then widen. <div class="dialogue">"Tu bhi?"<span class="gloss-en">You too?</span></div>

"Not a boyfriend. Something else. I can't explain."

Five seconds of study. Then she steps aside. "Come in."

She draws a quick map on the back of a pharmacology worksheet. <div class="dialogue">"Mess side door near the water cooler. Left, past the storage room, through the kitchen. Window at the back wall above the sink, opens outward, latch broken   just push. Alley two metres wide, then a chest-height compound wall. One camera, above the main entrance. Kitchen area   none. Budget."</div>

<div class="dialogue">"Ganesh's chai break: 7:30, front gate, two cups, phone, back at 8:00 sharp. Seven days I've timed it. More reliable than Indian Railways."</div>

You fold the map into your kameez pocket beside the tissue packet.

<div class="dialogue">"Whatever you're doing   be careful. Mrs. Joshi isn't stupid. And if Colonel Kulkarni's people are involved..."</div>

"They're the reason. Not the audience."

She nods. Doesn't ask more. The hostel code.

<<run setup.flag("anjali_visited")>>
<<run setup.flag("kitchen_route_detailed")>>
<<run setup.addItem("kitchen_route_map")>>
<<run setup.stat("rel_anjali", 3, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: kavya_calls_arjun_d2
<<img "kavya_calling_arjun.png" "Room 304, the call">>
<<if setup.has("full_briefing_done")>>\
Priya's out   //Sasural Simar Ka// rerun. Meera takes up her listening position by the door.

You call the number saved as "VIT Library Reference Desk." He answers before the first ring finishes; his hand was on the phone. You've already laid the whole thing out for him once tonight, from your side of it   the manuscript, the ritual, the deal. This call is shorter.

"Just confirming. 5 AM, Pataleshwar. Bring the bike   I need it for VIT. And Arjun: the rules stand. You will respect my body."

<div class="dialogue">"I will. I swear it. And you'll respect mine."</div>

"Deal. Don't be late. I am doing this against every rational principle I hold."

You hang up. Your hands are trembling. Meera takes one and squeezes. //"Thank you,"// she whispers   an ocean in two words.

"Thank me when your boyfriend navigates a bra hook without dislocating his shoulder."

She laughs, rare and unguarded.

<<run setup.flag("kavya_called_arjun")>>
<<run setup.stat("rel_arjun", 5, "kavya")>>
<<run setup.stat("rel_meera", 3, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>
<<else>>\
Priya leaves at 8:15   //Sasural Simar Ka// rerun, the common room, the siren call of a show so melodramatic it makes real life feel understated. Meera moves to the door. Listening position. The sentinel.

You pull out the Redmi. Arjun's number is saved as "VIT Library Reference Desk" because even your contact list has a cover story.

He picks up before the first ring completes. His hand was on the phone. The desperation in that instant is very human   and you're about to ask this very human boy to trust you with something that isn't human at all.

<div class="dialogue">"I'm going to explain something and you're going to think I'm pagal. Don't interrupt."</div>

You tell him. Methodically. The manuscript   age, origin, Nath Sampradaya. The ritual   kumkum circle, kalava thread, sixteen-syllable mantra, 108 repetitions. The test swap with Meera   ninety seconds in another body, the wrench, the blackout, waking up looking at your own face across the bed.

Clinical delivery. Facts first. The magic can take care of itself.

<div class="dialogue">"Tum pagal ho."<span class="gloss-en">You're crazy.</span></div>

"Probably. But it works. You swap with me. You come into the hostel in my body. Meera gets her boyfriend. I go out in your body   to VIT."

The room, on his end, goes quiet. Then: "Kab?"<span class="gloss-en">When?</span>

"Tomorrow. 5 AM. Pataleshwar Temple. Bring your bike   I'll need it to get to VIT."

<div class="dialogue">"You'll be in my body. Riding my Pulsar. It has a manual clutch. Release it too fast in first gear and it'll buck and throw you."</div>

"Then teach me before the swap."

<div class="dialogue">"Deal."</div>

The word comes fast. His body decided before his brain.

"Arjun." You soften   one degree. "We have rules. Your girlfriend helped write them. You will respect my body."

<div class="dialogue">"I will. I swear."</div>

"And I'll respect yours. 5 AM. Pataleshwar. Don't be late   I'm not a morning person and I'm doing this against every rational principle I hold."

You hang up. Your hands are trembling. Meera takes one and squeezes.

<div class="dialogue">"Thank you," she whispers. There's an ocean in those two words.</div>

"Don't thank me yet. Thank me when your boyfriend navigates a bra hook without dislocating his shoulder."

She laughs   rare and beautiful, unguarded.

<<run setup.flag("kavya_called_arjun")>>
<<run setup.flag("full_briefing_done")>>
<<run setup.stat("rel_arjun", 8, "kavya")>>
<<run setup.stat("rel_meera", 3, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>
<</if>>


:: kavya_shower_d2
<div class="intimate"><span class="tag">Intimate · body awareness</span>

Same stall. Same curtain that doesn't reach the floor. Same showerhead that spits like an old man. But today you //notice.//

The water on your skin, felt differently because you know it's one of your last showers in this body for a while. The weight of your breasts under the stream when you don't hold them. The curve where waist meets hip. The dark hair between your thighs.

You're not aroused. You're archiving. Memorising the coordinates of your body like backing up a hard drive before a migration.

Four minutes. The towel, once green, now the colour of doubt. The Clovia underwire   three hooks, second row, seven years of muscular scripture. The maroon salwar kameez. The dupatta pinned to minimise what Amma tactfully calls "shape."

<div class="thought kavya">Tomorrow, none of this. No hooks. No underwire. No drawstring. A flat chest, muscled maybe, and something between your legs you've only held in latex gloves over a cadaver.</div>
</div>

<<run setup.stat("energy", 3, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>
